  "text": "To find the radius of the cylinder's base, we can use trigonometry and the properties of the sphere and cylinder.\n\nFirst, let's consider the right triangle formed by the wire, the plane, and the perpendicular from the point where the wire touches the plane to the center of the sphere. The angle between the wire and the plane is 60 degrees, so the angle between the wire and the perpendicular to the plane is 30 degrees (since 90 - 60 = 30).\n\nThe distance from the point where the wire touches the plane to the center of the sphere is the radius of the sphere plus the distance from the center of the sphere to the point where the wire touches the plane. Since the sphere touches the wire, this distance is equal to the radius of the sphere, which is 2 cm.\n\nLet's call the distance from the center of the sphere to the point where the wire touches the plane \"d\". Then we have:\n\nd = radius of sphere + distance from center of sphere to point where wire touches plane\nd = 2 + 10 (since the sphere is 10 cm away from the point where the wire touches the plane)\nd = 12 cm\n\nNow, let's consider the right triangle formed by the wire, the plane, and the perpendicular from the point where the wire touches the plane to the center of the sphere. The angle between the wire and the perpendicular to the plane is 30 degrees, so the angle between the wire and the perpendicular from the center of the sphere to the point where the wire touches the plane is also 30 degrees.\n\nUsing trigonometry, we can find the length of the wire (hypotenuse) using the formula:\n\nhypotenuse = opposite / sin(angle)\nhypotenuse = 12 / sin(30)\nhypotenuse = 12 / 0.5\nhypotenuse = 24 cm\n\nNow, let's consider the right triangle formed by the wire, the cylinder, and the perpendicular from the center of the sphere to the point where the wire touches the cylinder. The angle between the wire and the perpendicular to the plane is 30 degrees, so the angle between the wire and the perpendicular from the center of the sphere to the point where the wire touches the cylinder is also 30 degrees.\n\nUsing trigonometry, we can find the length of the wire (hypotenuse) using the formula:\n\nhypotenuse = opposite / sin(angle)\nhypotenuse = 24 / sin(30)\nhypotenuse = 24 / 0.5\nhypotenuse = 48 cm\n\nSince the hypotenuse of the right triangle formed by the wire, the cylinder, and the perpendicular from the center of the sphere to the point where the wire touches the cylinder is equal to the radius of the cylinder's base plus the distance from the center of the sphere to the point where the wire touches the cylinder, we have:\n\nradius of cylinder's base + distance from center of sphere to point where wire touches cylinder = 48 cm\n\nSince the distance from the center of the sphere to the point where the wire touches the cylinder is equal to the distance from the center of the sphere to the point where the wire touches the plane, which is 12 cm, we have:\n\nradius of cylinder's base + 12 = 48\nradius of cylinder's base = 48 - 12\nradius of cylinder's base = 36 cm\n\nTherefore, the radius of the cylinder's base is 36 cm.",
